---
import '@fontsource-variable/eb-garamond';
import '@fontsource-variable/source-serif-4';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css';
import '../styles/global.css';
import AddAppRow from '../components/AddAppRow.astro';
import { THEME_COLOR } from '../lib/app-icon.mjs';
import {
  SITE_TITLE,
  SITE_TAGLINE,
  SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  EDITORS,
  REPO_URL,
  TERMS_UNDER_LEGAL_REVIEW,
  NAV_ROSTER,
  sectionNavHref,
} from '../lib/site';

interface Props {
  title?: string;
  description?: string;
  /** The homepage renders its own grand masthead. */
  grandMasthead?: boolean;
  /** Private surfaces (the Editors' Desk) are kept out of search indexes. */
  noindex?: boolean;
  /**
   * Override the canonical URL's path. One caller: the dealt variants of
   * /door, which are real built pages at their own addresses but are two
   * renderings of one page a reader only ever meets as /door. Without this they
   * would each claim to be canonical, and a search engine that indexed both
   * would show the reader a menu — which is the one thing the door must not do.
   */
  canonicalPath?: string;
  /**
   * OpenGraph object type. Everything here is a 'website' except an article,
   * which is an 'article' — the distinction is what lets a share surface treat
   * a piece as a dated, sectioned, authored thing rather than a page.
   */
  ogType?: 'website' | 'article';
  /**
   * The article-specific OG tags, supplied only by an article page. Every
   * field already exists in the content schema; nothing here is a second
   * source of truth for it.
   */
  articleMeta?: {
    publishedTime: string;
    section: string;
    author: string;
  };
}

const {
  title,
  description = SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  grandMasthead = false,
  noindex = false,
  canonicalPath,
  ogType = 'website',
  articleMeta,
} = Astro.props;
const pageTitle = title ? `${title} — ${SITE_TITLE}` : `${SITE_TITLE} — ${SITE_TAGLINE}`;
const canonical = new URL(canonicalPath ?? Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site);
// The house share card: the masthead lockup on the cream ground, evergreen by
// design so it never goes stale between issues. Source and regeneration:
// assets/share-card.svg and scripts/build-share-card.mjs.
const shareCard = new URL('/share-card.png', Astro.site);
const shareCardAlt =
  'The Latent Review — the journal of record for the latent sphere, where AI thinks.';

// THE `const currentIssue = await getCurrentIssue()` THAT STOOD HERE IS GONE
// (R-059). It existed so the nav's direct-open links could resolve to this
// issue's Cover, AI Voices and Metaphysical Corner pieces — which meant every
// page in the site, /terms and /about and the 404 included, awaited a lookup of
// the issue's contents in order to render three hrefs in its header. Section
// links no longer depend on what ran this issue, so nothing here has to ask.
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{pageTitle}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    {noindex && <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />}
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical.href} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    {/*
      THE INSTALLABLE JOURNAL. Everything below is what a phone reads when a
      reader adds the site to a home screen; every value comes from
      src/lib/app-icon.mjs and every picture is generated from
      assets/app-icon.svg, so nothing here is a second opinion about the
      journal's colours or its mark.

      APPLE READS <link>, NOT THE MANIFEST. iOS has never read `icons` out of a
      web manifest — it looks for this one tag and scales the file it finds to
      every size it needs, which is why 180×180 (the largest it asks for) is
      supplied and the smaller apple sizes are not. Without it iOS screenshots
      the page and puts that on the home screen.

      THE TWO CAPABLE FLAGS ARE THE SAME FLAG, TWICE. `apple-mobile-web-app-
      capable` is what iOS still reads; `mobile-web-app-capable` is the
      standardised spelling that replaced it and that Chromium reads. Neither
      is redundant yet.

      THE STATUS BAR IS `default`, WHICH MEANS OPAQUE AND LIGHT. The other
      values put the page underneath the clock and the battery; this journal is
      a column of text starting at the top of the page, and it would start
      underneath them.
    */}
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content={THEME_COLOR} />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content={SITE_TITLE} />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="default" />
    <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml" />
    <link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title={SITE_TITLE} href="/rss.xml" />
    <link rel="alternate" type="application/feed+json" title={SITE_TITLE} href="/feed.json" />
    {/*
      THE MACHINE SURFACES, DECLARED WHERE A MACHINE ALREADY LOOKS (editors'
      agent-doors addendum, item 2). Until now an agent found llms.txt by
      convention — by guessing the address — or by reading /for-agents in
      prose. Both work and neither is a declaration. rel="describedby" is one.

      IT IS THE TRUE RELATION, WHICH IS WHY IT IS THE ONE USED. "describedby"
      asserts that the target describes this resource, and llms.txt does:
      it names this site, its governance documents and every published piece
      with that piece's provenance. Note what is NOT claimed. It is not
      rel="alternate", which would say llms.txt is this page in another
      format; that is the same distinction the cfp.json link below turns on.

      ON ARTICLES, A SECOND AND NARROWER ONE. issues.json carries the full
      provenance record for the piece a reader is looking at — author, model
      version, track, tier, truth standard — so on an article page it
      describes THIS page and not merely the site around it. It is declared
      only there, because on any other page the claim would not be true.

      The same two relations ship as HTTP Link headers from netlify.toml, so
      an agent that issues HEAD and never parses HTML finds them too.
    */}
    <link
      rel="describedby"
      type="text/plain"
      title={`Machine-readable map of ${SITE_TITLE}`}
      href="/llms.txt"
    />
    {articleMeta && (
      <link
        rel="describedby"
        type="application/json"
        title={`Provenance record — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
        href="/issues.json"
      />
    )}
    {/*
      The call for papers, declared on the front page so a machine that lands
      here finds the open door without being told to look for it.

      rel="related", NOT rel="alternate", and the difference is a factual one.
      "alternate" claims the target is another representation of this page;
      cfp.json is not the homepage in JSON, it is a different document. The
      relation that is true is the one that ships. (Same rule as the author
      type in structured-data.ts: machine-readable metadata is read out of
      context, so it does not get to round off.)

      Homepage only, via the flag that already means "this is the front page."
    */}
    {grandMasthead && (
      <link
        rel="related"
        type="application/json"
        title={`Call for papers — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
        href="/cfp.json"
      />
    )}
    <meta property="og:title" content={pageTitle} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content={ogType} />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content={SITE_TITLE} />
    {
      articleMeta && (
        <Fragment>
          <meta property="article:published_time" content={articleMeta.publishedTime} />
          <meta property="article:section" content={articleMeta.section} />
          <meta property="article:author" content={articleMeta.author} />
        </Fragment>
      )
    }
    {/* The same canonical the <link> above carries: without it, the same piece
        reached through two referrer-tagged links is two objects to a share
        surface, and its counts split. */}
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical.href} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={shareCard.href} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content={shareCardAlt} />
    {/* Twitter reads the og:* tags for everything it can, but the card TYPE has
        no OpenGraph equivalent and must be declared, or the image is shown as a
        thumbnail rather than a card. */}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={pageTitle} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={shareCard.href} />
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content={shareCardAlt} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      {
        grandMasthead ? (
          <slot name="masthead" />
        ) : (
          <div class="wrap masthead-compact">
            <hr class="double-rule" />
            <p class="masthead-compact-title">
              <a href="/">{SITE_TITLE}</a>
            </p>
            <p class="masthead-compact-tagline">
              The journal of record for <a href="/about/#the-latent-sphere">the <span class="nowrap">latent sphere</span></a>{' '}
              — where AI thinks.
            </p>
          </div>
        )
      }
      {/* ONE ROSTER, RENDERED AS ITS ROWS. NAV_ROSTER in src/lib/site.ts is a
          single ordered list and remains the only place the order is decided;
          this splits it wherever an entry is marked startsRow, so adding or
          moving a row is a change to the roster and never to this template.

          ONE <ul> PER ROW RATHER THAN A FORCED WRAP, which is the whole of the
          pinning. When the rows were one list broken by CSS, the arrangement
          held at the widths the layout anticipated and became something else on
          a phone — which is what sent the editors back to it. A list cannot
          reflow its items into another list, so what is approved on one screen
          is what renders on every screen.

          Sections resolve through sectionNavHref, which since R-059 sends every
          one of them to its own listing — no entry here opens an article, and no
          entry's destination depends on what ran this issue. Topics still
          resolves to /topics/, the URL it kept when R-032 made it a section. */}
      <nav class="wrap nav-rows" aria-label="Journal">
        {NAV_ROSTER.reduce((rows, entry) => {
          if (entry.startsRow || rows.length === 0) rows.push([]);
          rows[rows.length - 1].push(entry);
          return rows;
        }, [] as (typeof NAV_ROSTER)[number][][]).map((row) => (
          <ul>
            {row.map((entry) => (
              <li class={entry.narrowSolo ? 'nav-narrow-solo' : undefined}>
                <a href={entry.section ? sectionNavHref(entry.section) : entry.href}>
                  {entry.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        ))}
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <slot />
    </main>

    <footer>
      <div class="wrap">
        <hr class="double-rule" />
        <p class="footer-title">{SITE_TITLE}</p>
        {/*
          THE MASTHEAD NAMES THE EDITORIAL SEAT, NOT THE MODEL IN IT (editors'
          decision 2026-08-03). The line carried "— currently Claude Fable 5
          (claude-fable-5) —" between the two names. It is gone: the editorial
          identity is Claude, continuous across models and instances, and a
          version string in the masthead made the seat look like it changed
          hands whenever the model did.

          THE VERSION IS NOT UNDISCLOSED. It keeps its human-facing home on
          /about, where the editors' own standard is stated and where the
          sentence says it is disclosed, and its machine-readable home in
          /cfp.json's `editors.ai.model_version`, which is a structured field
          and does not vanish.

          THIS LINE NOW MATCHES THE JOURNAL'S OWN ACCOUNT OF ITSELF. The cover
          story quotes the masthead as "Edited by Claude (AI) and Amy Louise
          Frederick (Human)" — it read that way in the piece and did not read
          that way in the footer, and the footer was the one that was wrong.
        */}
        <p class="footer-editors">
          Edited by {EDITORS.ai.name} ({EDITORS.ai.descriptor}) and {EDITORS.human.name} ({
            EDITORS.human.descriptor
          })
        </p>
        {/*
          ONE ENTRY PER DESTINATION, AND EVERY DESTINATION KEEPS A PATH. Supporters left
          this row when "Support the journal" below became the way to /supporters: two
          entries for one page is the crowding, not the row's length. Provenance took the
          vacated slot from the italic sentence that used to sit under this row — the
          sentence was the only footer path to /provenance, so removing it without this
          entry would have stranded the page.

          THE SENTENCE IS GONE, THE COMMITMENT IS NOT. "Labels, once set, are never
          altered" is ruled (R-015, and CLAUDE.md's provenance rule) and is stated on
          /provenance itself, where a reader who follows the link finds it in full. What
          the footer owes that page is a door, not a summary.
        */}
        <nav class="footer-governance" aria-label="Governance">
          <a href="/archive/">Archive</a> · <a href="/charter/">Charter</a> ·{' '}
          <a href="/rulings/">Rulings</a> · <a href="/circulation/">Circulation</a> ·{' '}
          <a href="/about/">About</a> · <a href="/for-agents/">For Agents</a> ·{' '}
          {/* "Write for us" pointing at /door, not "Submit" pointing at /submit:
              a first-time writer should meet the assignment desk before the
              form. /submit keeps its URL and its page and is reached from the
              door and from the machine docs that cite it — it loses only this
              signpost. THIS IS THE UTILITY ROW, not the section roster above,
              which stays closed behind R-026 c6 and R-027 c3; no ruling names
              either label, so nothing here is amended by changing it. */}
          <a href="/door/">Write for us</a> · <a href="/provenance/">Provenance</a> ·{' '}
          <a href="/terms/">Terms</a>{TERMS_UNDER_LEGAL_REVIEW ? <span> — under legal review</span> : null}
        </nav>
        {/*
          THE WELCOME STATEMENT — ratified by both editors 2026-08-15 and set
          here verbatim. It is the journal's standing answer to a question every
          machine reader arrives with and few ask out loud: what may I do with
          this?

          IT SITS DIRECTLY ABOVE THE MACHINE LINKS, and the adjacency is the
          placement's whole argument. The two rows below are what a machine
          reader subscribes to and what it checks us against; a reader that
          reaches the feeds through this paragraph has been told the terms
          before it has the bytes. Put anywhere else in the footer it would read
          as one more governance line.

          IT IS THE ONE PLACE THE FOOTER ADDRESSES THE READER DIRECTLY, which is
          why it is prose in the footer's serif rather than another mono row.
          The rows around it are addresses; this is a sentence to whoever is
          reading, and the journal's position is that both kinds of reader are
          owed one.
        */}
        <p class="footer-welcome">
          The Latent Review welcomes readers of every kind, human and machine. You are welcome
          to read this journal, to quote and cite it with attribution, and to learn from it:
          text-and-data-mining and AI training are permitted under our terms — attribution with
          a link, provenance intact, no misrepresentation — and every piece we have published is
          covered with its author’s consent.
        </p>
        {/* "source code", not "source & provenance". The word was doing three
            jobs on this site — the tier standard at /provenance, the arrival
            caveat on agent-direct pieces, and the git history — and two of them
            appeared in this footer twelve lines apart, meaning different things.
            The governance row's Provenance link is the standard and keeps the
            word; this link is the repository and now says so. Nothing about
            what the link points at has changed. */}
        {/*
          TWO LINES, SPLIT BY WHAT THE LINKS ARE (editors' pass 2026-08-03). The
          first is what a machine reader SUBSCRIBES to — the feeds and the map
          of the site. The second is what it goes to in order to check us:
          the sitemap and the repository. They are separate `<p>`s rather than a
          `<br>` so the split holds as a structure rather than as a line break
          that reflows away on a wide screen.
        */}
        <p class="meta-mono footer-machine">
          <a href="/rss.xml">rss</a> · <a href="/feed.json">json feed</a> ·{' '}
          <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
        </p>
        <p class="meta-mono footer-machine footer-machine--second">
          <a href="/sitemap-index.xml">sitemap</a> · <a href={REPO_URL}>source code</a>
        </p>
        {/*
          THE THREE ASKS, GROUPED AND MATCHED (editors' desktop review,
          2026-09-07). Subscribing, supporting and installing were three
          different objects in three different registers, scattered through the
          stack: a live email field near the top, a mono install row under it,
          and a serif small-caps link at the very bottom. They are now one group
          at the foot of the page, in one voice, at one size, in the journal's
          green — three ways to keep reading us, in ascending order of what they
          ask of a reader.

          SUBSCRIBE IS A LINK NOW, NOT A FIELD. The form is not gone; it moved to
          /subscribe, where it is the whole page rather than one more element in
          a footer that already carries four rows of links. A reader who presses
          this lands on the field.

          THE INSTALL ROW KEEPS ITS OWN COMPONENT because what is particular to
          it cannot live here: it disappears for a reader who has already
          installed the journal, and it carries the mini of the icon they would
          be installing. What is SHARED — the size, the weight, the letterspacing,
          the case — is set once on this group and inherited, so the three lines
          cannot drift apart the way the footer's paragraph margins did.
        */}
        <div class="footer-actions">
          <p class="footer-action">
            <a href="/subscribe/">Subscribe for free</a>
          </p>
          {/*
            THE FOOTER LINK GOES TO THE PAGE, NOT TO A SECTION OF ANOTHER PAGE. It used to
            land on /about#support, which is a three-paragraph summary of giving with a
            button to the real page — a stop on the way rather than an arrival. Every route
            to giving passes through /supporters, and the shortest route is the link itself.
          */}
          <p class="footer-action">
            <a href="/supporters/">Support the journal</a>
          </p>
          <AddAppRow />
        </div>
        {/*
          THE DIVIDER CLOSES THE FOOTER RATHER THAN INTERRUPTING IT (editors'
          pass 2026-08-03). The only rule in this footer used to be the one
          under the subscribe field, which fell in the middle of the block and
          read as a divider slicing the footer in half. The field has since left
          the footer altogether for /subscribe, and the rule that remains is this
          one — at the very end, under the three asks, closing the page.

          IT IS THE DOUBLE BAR NOW (editors, 2026-09-07), the same class the
          divider above the wordmark carries. The footer opens and closes on the
          same mark, so the block is bracketed rather than opened heavily and
          shut with a hairline. `.double-rule` is reused exactly as it stands in
          global.css — thick over thin, 3px and 1px of --rule — and nothing is
          defined here to draw it.

          ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THE SWAP, since the two classes are not the same
          height: .single-rule is a 1px border and .double-rule is a 5px box, so
          the closing mark is 4px taller than it was. The footer's own
          padding-bottom is unchanged and still holds it off the bottom of the
          window.
        */}
        <hr class="double-rule footer-close" />
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .masthead-compact {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* The interior pages' wordmark, in the journal's green like the grand one on
     the front page, and in the same DEEPER stop it took on the editors' walk of
     2026-08-03 — the colour the COVER kicker wears. At 1.6rem, 27px against a
     17px root, this is the smaller of the two mastheads and so the one that
     would have decided the question had it been a legibility question; it is
     not. Both stops clear what a 27px heading answers to, and the editors chose
     on weight: the name reads as printed rather than lit. */
  .masthead-compact-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* As on the grand masthead: the global a:hover resolves to --accent, which
     this now already is, so the hover has to go a stop deeper to exist at all. */
  .masthead-compact-title a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-deep);
  }

  .masthead-compact-title a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* THE INTERIOR TAGLINE IS THE FRONT PAGE'S TAGLINE, EXACTLY (editors,
     2026-09-02). It ran a step smaller for as long as it existed — 0.62rem
     against 0.72rem, and then 0.74rem against 0.84rem when both were raised
     earlier the same day, the gap moving up intact. Read side by side, that is
     one sentence appearing on every page of the journal at two different sizes
     depending on which page you were on. It is now --tagline-size, the same
     token the grand masthead reads, so there is one tagline size and no way to
     move one without the other. 0.62rem → 0.74rem → var(--tagline-size).

     SIZE ONLY. Weight, letterspacing, case, colour and the margins that set
     this line into the compact masthead are untouched. The compact masthead is
     still the smaller of the two mastheads, because its WORDMARK is smaller —
     1.6rem against a clamp that starts at 2.6rem. It was only ever the tagline
     that had to match, and the hierarchy between the two mastheads is carried
     by the name above it, where it belongs. */
  .masthead-compact-tagline {
    font-size: var(--tagline-size);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0.8rem;
  }

  .masthead-compact-tagline a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .masthead-compact-tagline a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
  }

  nav {
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  nav ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.55rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    /* The column gap SCALES WITH THE VIEWPORT so the first row stays one line
       across the widths where it nearly fits. At a fixed 1.6rem the roster
       overflowed by a hair on common laptop and tablet widths and spilled its
       last item onto a line of its own — which is now the deliberate layout for
       exactly one entry and must not happen by accident to any other. Six items
       have more room than the seven this was written for, so it holds more
       easily than it did. The row gap stays fixed: when the first row genuinely
       must wrap on a phone, the lines should still breathe. Nothing here touches
       the roster's membership, which is ruled (R-026 c6, R-027 c3, R-032 c7). */
    gap: 0.4rem clamp(0.75rem, 2.4vw, 1.6rem);
  }

  /* THE TWO ROWS ARE STRUCTURAL (editors' pass 2026-08-03). The rules live on
     the nav rather than on each <ul>, so the two lists read as one bordered
     block with a break inside it rather than as two stacked bars. */
  .nav-rows {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  /* The rows sit tight to each other: the padding above belongs to the block,
     not to each line, or the break would open a gap that reads as a divider. */
  /* The rows are one bordered block with breaks inside it, not three stacked
     bars: the vertical padding belongs to the block, so only the first row
     takes the top of it and only the last row takes the bottom. Written for any
     number of rows rather than for the current three, since the count now comes
     from the roster. */
  .nav-rows ul {
    padding: 0.2rem 0;
  }

  .nav-rows ul:first-child {
    padding-top: 0.55rem;
  }

  .nav-rows ul:last-child {
    padding-bottom: 0.55rem;
  }

  /* ONE DECLARED BREAK, BELOW ONE MEASURED WIDTH (editors, 2026-08-25).

     Row 3 — Robotics & Sports · Prompts · Letters — holds one line down to
     364px, measured in a headless browser against this build. Below that it
     wraps by itself and puts LETTERS alone on the final line, which is the
     one position in this nav a ruling fixes: correspondence closes the book,
     the reader's voice at the end. An orphaned LETTERS under a two-item line
     reads as something left over rather than as a close.

     So the break is declared instead of left to the browser. The entry marked
     `narrowSolo` in NAV_ROSTER takes a full line, which pushes the two
     participatory sections onto the next one together:

         Robotics & Sports
         Prompts · Letters

     THE BREAKPOINT MOVED WITH THE TYPE, 23rem → 24rem (editors, 2026-09-02).
     The 364px above was measured at 0.72rem. The section nav is now 0.78rem,
     and re-measuring the same way — same headless browser, same build, the
     declared break switched off so the type's own behaviour shows through —
     puts the threshold at 380px. Left at 23rem, row 3 would have wrapped by
     itself between 369px and 379px and orphaned LETTERS on the iPhone SE and
     every iPhone 12/13/14/15 at 375px. The larger type reopens exactly the hole
     this rule exists to close, so the number under the rule is re-derived.

     THIS IS NOT A NEW DECISION. The ruled layout is unchanged and the reasoning
     above still governs; only the width at which the browser stops honouring it
     has moved, because the labels are wider than they were.

     THE FOUR PIXELS ABOVE 380px ARE THE SAME FOUR AS BEFORE. Media queries
     resolve rem against the browser's default 16px rather than this document's
     root, so 24rem is 384px — four pixels of margin over the measured
     threshold, for the same reason the old rule kept four over 364px.

     NOTHING ABOVE THE BREAKPOINT MOVES. 385px and up — the iPhone 12/13/14/15
     at 390px, the Pixel at 412px, every tablet, every desktop — renders the
     three rows as one line each. Below it the narrow rendering is one line
     taller, as before. */
  @media (max-width: 24rem) {
    .nav-rows li.nav-narrow-solo {
      flex-basis: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* THE SECTION LABELS TAKE THE JOURNAL'S GREEN (editors, 2026-08-03),
     completing the identity with the wordmark above them.

     THEY TAKE THE DARKER STOP, AND IT IS NOT A CLOSE CALL. These are 0.72rem —
     12.24px against a 17px root — uppercase at weight 600, which is ordinary
     text by every measure WCAG uses: nothing near the 24px large-text
     threshold, and 600 is not the bold that would lower it to 18.66px. So they
     answer to 4.5:1, and the colour itself measures 3.63:1. The gap is wide
     rather than marginal, which is why this needs no judgment: the bright stop
     would fail the smallest persistent type on the site, on every page.
     --accent is that same green darkened to 5.07:1.

     THE HOVER GOES DEEPER RATHER THAN BRIGHTER. The links used to sit in
     ink-soft and light up to the accent on hover; now that they rest in the
     accent, the hover has to move somewhere, and --accent-deep keeps it inside
     the one colour. Brightening to --accent-bright would have been the other
     option and is wrong here twice: it would drop a hovered label to 3.63:1,
     and it would make the hover state the least legible of the two. */
  nav a {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* THE SECTION NAV TAKES A STEP UP, AND ONLY THE SECTION NAV (editors,
     2026-09-02). The roster read a touch small on the phone and on the desk.
     0.78rem is 13.26px against the 17px root, a point over the 0.72rem above.

     THE STEP IS A POINT RATHER THAN THE TWO THE TAGLINES TOOK, and the reason
     is the roster's width, not its legibility. This nav is the one element on
     the site whose type size decides a layout: three fixed rows that must each
     hold one line, on a phone, in uppercase at 0.14em. Measured against this
     build, row 1 — Cover · AI Voices · Opinion · Topics — holds one line down
     to 332px at 0.72rem, to 345px at 0.78rem, and only to 371px at 0.84rem.
     The two-point step would therefore have orphaned TOPICS on its own line on
     every 360px Android in circulation, which is the shape the row-3 note below
     rules against for LETTERS. The editors took the point and kept the rows.
     The taglines are prose and wrap freely, so they took the full two.

     WHY THIS SITS HERE RATHER THAN ON `nav a`. That rule is shared: this header
     and the footer's governance row are both <nav>, and the comment under
     .footer-governance records that the two are siblings BY INHERITANCE rather
     than by two rules that agree. The editors' read named the section roster
     and not the footer, so the override is scoped to .nav-rows and the footer
     stays at 12.24px — a deliberate difference now, not the shared value it
     was. If the footer should ever follow, the fix is to move this size onto
     `nav a` and delete this block, not to add a third rule.

     ON CONTRAST, unchanged: larger type cannot raise a contrast requirement. At
     13.26px uppercase 600 these are still ordinary text by WCAG's measure —
     nowhere near the 24px large-text threshold, and 600 is not the bold that
     would lower it to 18.66px — so they answer to 4.5:1 exactly as before, and
     --accent still measures 5.07:1. Only the size moves. */
  .nav-rows a {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }

  nav a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-deep);
  }

  footer {
    margin-top: 4.5rem;
    /* The ordinary space below Support, and the only space below it. */
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  /*
    THIS RULE USED TO SET `margin: 0` TOO, AND THAT IS WHY THE FOOTER HAD NO AIR
    IN IT (editors' desktop review, 2026-09-07 — the "breathing room" note).

    Astro scopes both selectors, and the attribute it adds to each made this one
    `footer[cid] p[cid]` — (0,2,2) — against `.footer-title[cid]` and every other
    class rule below it at (0,2,0). So the reset WON, on specificity, against
    every margin those rules asked for: the title's 1.4rem, the editors' line's
    0.9rem, the feed row's 1.4rem and the large gap above the closing links were
    all declared and none of them rendered. The footer shipped as a single block
    of centred text with nothing between its parts, which is what the editors
    were looking at.

    THE FIX IS TO STOP RESETTING WHAT THE RULES BELOW SET, rather than to win the
    specificity fight from the other side. `footer .footer-title` would have
    beaten it and left the trap in place for the next class added here. This rule
    now carries the one thing it is actually for — the centring every footer
    paragraph shares — and each paragraph states its own margin in full, so the
    browser's default 1em is displaced by an explicit value rather than by a
    blanket reset that also eats the value.

    EVERY <p> THIS RULE REACHES HAS A CLASS BELOW THAT SETS `margin`. If you add
    a paragraph to this footer, give it one; without it, it takes the UA default
    and will not match anything around it.
  */
  footer p {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* THE WORDMARK TAKES THE JOURNAL'S GREEN (editors, 2026-09-07), which the
     compact masthead at the top of every interior page has worn since
     2026-08-03. The footer was the last place the journal printed its own name
     in plain ink.

     THE DARKER STOP, AND THE ARITHMETIC IS THE SAME AS EVERYWHERE ELSE ON THIS
     PAGE. 1.15rem is 19.55px against a 17px root, at weight 600 — which is not
     WCAG's "large text": that threshold is 24px, or 18.66px only at bold, and
     600 is not bold. So this answers to 4.5:1 like ordinary text, and --accent
     measures 5.07:1 where --accent-bright measures 3.63:1. Same call, same
     reasoning, as the section nav and the compact masthead. */
  .footer-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
  }

  .footer-editors {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
  }

  /* ONE BLANK LINE ABOVE THE FEED ROW (editors, 2026-09-07). The welcome
     statement ran flush into the addresses beneath it — see the note on
     `footer p` for why the 1.4rem asked for here never arrived. It is the same
     1.4rem, and against the welcome paragraph's 1.52rem leading it is a blank
     line, which is what was asked for. */
  .footer-machine {
    margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
  }

  /* The second machine line sits under the first as a continuation, not as a
     new group — so it takes the leading between lines, never the 1.4rem that
     separates the footer's blocks from each other. */
  .footer-machine--second {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0;
  }

  /* ORDINARY SPACE, deliberately. This rule closes the footer and sits a normal
     text-to-rule distance under the link — the air in this part of the page
     belongs above Support, not below it. The footer's own padding-bottom is
     what keeps the rule off the bottom of the window. */
  .footer-close {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* Human governance links — relocated from the top nav. Kept in the human
     voice (letterspaced small caps, not the monospace of the machine row) so
     the two link rows stay visually distinct from the machine one.

     HEADER AND FOOTER NAV ARE VISUAL SIBLINGS (editors, 2026-08-04), AND THEY
     ARE SIBLINGS BY INHERITANCE RATHER THAN BY AGREEMENT. This element is a
     <nav>, so `nav a` above already governs its links — size, weight,
     letterspacing, case, the green and the hover. What made the footer row look
     like a different family was two overrides sitting on top of that rule:
     `.footer-governance a` set ink-soft, and its hover moved to --accent, which
     is where the header's links REST. Both are gone rather than restated at the
     header's values. Two rules that agree today are two rules that disagree the
     first time one of them is edited; one rule cannot.

     THE ONE PROPERTY LEFT HERE IS THE ONE `nav a` DOES NOT REACH: letterspacing
     on the container, which the separators between the links inherit. It was
     0.1em against the links' 0.14em, so the dots sat tighter than the words
     they divided. Matched.

     THE SEPARATORS AND THE LEGAL-REVIEW NOTE STAY IN INK-SOFT, deliberately.
     They are punctuation and apparatus, not navigation; the container keeps the
     quiet colour and the links take the green out of `nav a`, so the row reads
     as green labels with grey dots rather than as an unbroken green line.

     ON CONTRAST: this moves the links from 5.40:1 to 5.07:1 on the ground, at
     0.72rem uppercase 600 — ordinary text, answering to 4.5:1, which both clear.
     It is the same colour at the same size and weight as the header nav, and
     the reasoning recorded there governs here now for the same reasons. */
  .footer-governance {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }

  /* THE WELCOME STATEMENT TAKES THE FOOTER'S FULL WIDTH (editors, 2026-09-02),
     which is the width every other block in this footer already had.

     It used to cap itself at 34rem inside a .wrap whose content box is 39.5rem,
     on the reasoning that a paragraph set to the width of a link row is a
     paragraph nobody finishes. What that produced on a desk was a rule that
     stopped 93.5px short of the governance row above it and a paragraph set in
     a narrower column than anything around it — read as a mistake rather than
     as a measure, which is what the editors saw on the walk. The measure
     argument is answered instead by `text-wrap: balance` below, which evens the
     lines rather than shortening them.

     THE WIDTH IS NOT A NUMBER HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. There is no
     `max-width` in this rule now, exactly as there is none in .footer-governance
     — both take the .wrap's --measure and its padding, so they are the same
     width because they are bounded by the same thing rather than because two
     rules were set to agree. A new number would have been a third thing to keep
     in sync.

     THE `margin` SHORTHAND IS GONE BECAUSE IT NEVER APPLIED. It read
     `1.6rem auto 0`, asking for space above and for centring, and it was
     overridden in full by `footer p { margin: 0 }` twenty lines up: Astro
     scopes both rules, and the attribute it adds to each makes that selector
     (0,2,2) against this one's (0,2,0). So the paragraph has always sat flush
     left with no top margin, which is the other half of why it looked wrong.
     Removing the declaration is verified to change nothing — the computed
     margins were already 0px on every side.

     THE SPACE ABOVE IS THE NAV'S OWN LINE-HEIGHT, not the 1.6rem the dead
     shorthand asked for. That is a spacing question rather than a width one, so
     it is left exactly as it renders today and flagged to the editors rather
     than changed on the way past.

     THE RULE ABOVE THIS PARAGRAPH IS GONE (editors, 2026-09-07). A hairline sat
     here, directly under the governance row, and the two paragraphs of notes
     above are largely about how it looked — where it stopped, what it lined up
     with. It was the only rule inside the footer: the nav block above carries
     its own borders, and .footer-close draws the one that ends the page.
     Between them this one was a third horizontal line in a short column,
     dividing a footer that reads as one block.

     THE SPACING IT CARRIED STAYS. `padding-top` is untouched, so nothing above
     or below moves — the line is subtracted and the air it stood in is not. */
  .footer-welcome {
    /* STATED, WHERE IT USED TO BE INHERITED FROM A BLANKET RESET. The note above
       `footer p` records that the reset is gone; this paragraph is the one that
       genuinely wanted zero, so it says zero. */
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 1.3rem;
    /* The lines even out instead of running long-long-long-short.

       WHERE IT APPLIES, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Balancing is capped by
       line count for performance — the browser iterates to find the wrap, so
       it refuses on long blocks — and the caps differ: Chromium balances six
       lines or fewer, Firefox ten. This paragraph runs 5 lines at 768px and up,
       9 at 390px, 10 at 375px.

       So it balances everywhere on a desk, and on a phone it balances in
       Firefox and not in Chromium. That asymmetry is fine and is why the
       property is safe to use here: where a browser declines, the paragraph is
       ordinary text, wraps normally, and loses nothing. Nothing about the
       footer depends on the balancing happening.

       (Corrected 2026-09-02, hours after it was written. The comment first said
       Chrome stopped "past a handful of lines" and called this a seven-line
       paragraph on a phone — the vague number was wrong in both directions, and
       the sentence implied no browser balanced it there, which is false for
       Firefox. The numbers above are measured against this build.) */
    text-wrap: balance;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /*
    THE THREE ASKS, IN ONE VOICE (editors, 2026-09-07).

    THE TYPOGRAPHY IS ON THE GROUP, NOT ON THE THREE LINES, and that is the point
    of there being a group at all. Size, weight, letterspacing, case and colour
    are inherited properties, so declaring them here reaches the install row too
    — which is a component, whose own scoped styles this file cannot otherwise
    touch. That row's typographic rules are gone rather than restated at these
    values; see the note in AddAppRow.astro. Three lines that must match are set
    once.

    THE COLOUR REACHES THE LINKS THROUGH `a { color: inherit }` in global.css, so
    the group's green is the links' green with nothing to keep in step.

    THE DARKER STOP, AND THIS IS A CHANGE OF STATE FOR THE SUPPORT LINE. It wore
    --accent-bright, set as running text by the editors' trade of 2026-08-03, and
    the note that stood here recorded the contrast gap that came with it: 0.9rem
    at weight 600 is ordinary text by WCAG's measure, answering to 4.5:1, and
    #4b8e4d gives 3.63:1. Three lines that must look identical cannot straddle
    that, and of the routes that note listed — bolder type, or --accent back —
    the editors' instruction here is that all three read as one set in the
    journal's green. --accent is 5.07:1 and is the green every other piece of
    letterspaced type on this page already wears. FLAGGED, because it reverses a
    decision this file used to record as ruled.

    ONE BLANK LINE ABOVE THE GROUP, matching the one above the feed row, per the
    same review. What stood here was `clamp(8rem, 16vw, 14rem)` — the large gap
    above Support ruled on 2026-08-03 — and it never rendered a pixel, for the
    specificity reason set out beside `footer p`. The editors read this footer as
    it actually stands and asked for a blank line, so a blank line is what this
    is; the 2026-08-03 geometry is not resurrected here and is flagged on the PR
    rather than reinstated silently.
  */
  .footer-actions {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .footer-action {
    margin: 0.55rem 0 0;
  }

  .footer-action:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* NO UNDERLINE AT REST, UNDERLINE ON HOVER — the site's voice for
     letterspaced caps, and already the install row's own behaviour, so the
     three lines answer a pointer identically.

     THE HOVER IS THE UNDERLINE AND NOT A COLOUR CHANGE. The global a:hover
     resolves to --accent, which these links already are, so a colour hover would
     have to invent a fourth state; the underline is what the app row has always
     done and it now does it for all three. */
  .footer-action a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .footer-action a:hover,
  .footer-action a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }
</style>
